---
import { Clock } from "lucide-react";
---

<article class="space-y-8">
  <header class="space-y-4">
    <div class="flex items-center space-x-3 text-xs font-bold text-saffron-600 dark:text-saffron-400 uppercase tracking-widest font-latin">
      <span>Academic Prep (School)</span>
      <span>•</span>
      <span class="flex items-center space-x-1"><Clock class="w-3.5 h-3.5" /> <span>8 min read</span></span>
    </div>
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-5xl font-black text-charcoal dark:text-zinc-150 leading-tight font-latin">
      NCERT Sanskrit Board Exams: Proven Study & Writing Strategies
    </h1>
    <p class="text-base md:text-lg text-charcoal/70 dark:text-zinc-400 leading-relaxed font-semibold italic font-latin">
      A comprehensive roadmap to scoring a perfect 100/100 in CBSE Class 10 Sanskrit.
    </p>
  </header>

  <hr class="border-saffron-100 dark:border-zinc-800" />

  <div class="prose prose-saffron max-w-none dark:prose-invert text-charcoal/80 dark:text-zinc-350 space-y-6 leading-relaxed text-sm md:text-base font-latin">
    <h2>1. Understanding the Exam Pattern</h2>
    <p>
      Many students view Sanskrit as a challenging subject, but it is actually one of the most high-scoring subjects in the CBSE Board curriculum. Because Sanskrit is logically structured and mathematically precise, answers are either correct or incorrect—leaving no room for subjective marks deduction.
    </p>
    <p>
      The CBSE Class 10 Sanskrit paper is divided into four distinct sections:
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Section A: Reading Comprehension (Apahita Avabodhanam - 10 Marks):</strong> Unseen passage analysis.</li>
      <li><strong>Section B: Writing Skills (Rachanatmakam Karyam - 15 Marks):</strong> Letter writing, picture description, and translation.</li>
      <li><strong>Section C: Applied Grammar (Anupryukta Vyakaran - 25 Marks):</strong> Sandhi, Samasa, Pratyaya, time-telling, and sentence correction.</li>
      <li><strong>Section D: Textbook Comprehension (Pathita Avabodhanam - 30 Marks):</strong> Shloka interpretation, question formulation, and word-meanings from the Shemushi textbook.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>2. How to Tackle Applied Grammar (Section C)</h2>
    <p>
      This section represents 25 marks of pure mathematical scoring. One mistake leads to zero, but a correct answer guarantees full marks.
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
      <li>
        <strong>Sandhi:</strong> Focus heavily on the prescribed syllabus. For Class 10, master <em>Vyañjana Sandhi</em> (specifically Jaśtva) and <em>Visarga Sandhi</em> (specifically U-Sandhi and Lopa).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Samasa:</strong> Memorize the identifiers for the four major compounds: Tatpuruṣa (case-based), Dvandva (coordinating), Avyayībhāva (prefixed), and Bahuvrīhi (referential).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Pratyaya (Suffixes):</strong> Practice the suffixes <code>matup</code>, <code>thak</code>, <code>ktavatu</code>, and feminine suffixes <code>ṭāp</code> and <code>ṅīp</code>. Know how they modify base nouns.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>3. Master Writing Skills (Section B)</h2>
    <p>
      Section B tests your active translation and composition skills. Follow these tips to prevent silly grammatical errors:
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
      <li>
        <strong>Formal Letter Writing (Patralekhanam):</strong> The letter is presented as a fill-in-the-blanks grid with a help-box (Mañjūṣā). Before writing, read the entire letter to grasp the context. Pay close attention to verb tenses (Lakāra) and singular/plural matchings of subjects.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Picture Description (Citralekhanam):</strong> You must write 5 simple, grammatically correct sentences about a picture. Do not write complex sentences. Use standard templates like:
        <br />
        <code>अस्मिन् चित्रे [Noun in Locative/Subject] अस्ति/सन्ति।</code> (In this picture, there is/are...)
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>4. Study Plan & Resource Optimization</h2>
    <p>
      To achieve a perfect score, integrate these study habits into your schedule:
    </p>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Solve Sample Papers:</strong> Practice at least 5 previous years' CBSE papers. This trains you to manage time efficiently.</li>
      <li><strong>Use Interactive Study Labs:</strong> Utilize Sanskritbhashi's <strong>School Prep Module</strong> to practice chapter-by-chapter vocabulary and grammar. The diagnostic feedback loop helps isolate your weak points instantly.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>5. Conclusion</h2>
    <p>
      A score of 100/100 in Sanskrit is entirely achievable. By breaking the exam down into distinct sections, mastering basic grammar formulas, and utilizing Sanskritbhashi's school-prep practice cards, you can walk into your board exam with total confidence.
    </p>
  </div>
</article>
